cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "fabric-godot-service: the XR client for one zone of the multiplayer fabric"
abstract: >
  A Godot XR client for one zone of the V-Sekai multiplayer fabric. It opens a
  WebTransport session to fabric-physics-service, decodes back-to-back 100-byte
  XRGridEntityPacket slices into props and remote avatars, interpolates between
  the last two snapshots at headset frame rate, and sends this player's head and
  two hands back at 20 Hz. It simulates nothing: the service is authoritative
  over every prop, and the client's only output is its own tracked pose.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-12"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/fabric-godot-service"
keywords:
  - godot
  - openxr
  - webtransport
  - multiplayer
  - networked-physics
  - virtual-reality

# What this client is built on. It is almost entirely other people's decisions: an engine fork,
# a transport module inside it, a wire format proved elsewhere, and a service that owns the
# state. Saying so in one place beats a README that mentions each once.
references:

  - type: software
    title: "fabric-godot-core: the Godot engine fork, branch gyre"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/fabric-godot-core"
    notes: >
      The engine this runs on, built precision=double. Supplies modules/http3's
      WebTransportPeer over picoquic, and modules/xr_grid's XRGridEntityPacket
      and XRGridXROrigin. Must be the gyre branch: master is upstream Godot.

  - type: software
    title: "fabric-physics-service: server-authoritative networked physics for one ward"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/fabric-physics-service"
    notes: "The other end. It owns the state; this draws it."

  - type: software
    title: "lean-entity-packet: the 100-byte integral entity wire packet, proved"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/lean-entity-packet"
    notes: "The layout, and the reason no file here carries a field offset."

  - type: software
    title: "fabric-fanout-edge: interest-filtered fan-out, the leaf of the relay tree"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/fabric-fanout-edge"
    notes: >
      What writes the slices this decodes. Its README is where the batching
      contract lives: back-to-back records, count recovered as len / 100.

  - type: article
    title: "Networked Physics in Virtual Reality"
    authors:
      - family-names: Fiedler
        given-names: Glenn
    year: 2018
    url: "https://gafferongames.com/post/networked_physics_in_virtual_reality/"
    notes: >
      Where the three-object avatar comes from: a head and two hands driven by
      the tracked headset and touch controllers.

  - type: software
    title: "picoquic: a minimal QUIC implementation"
    authors:
      - family-names: Huitema
        given-names: Christian
    year: 2026
    license: MIT
    url: "https://github.com/private-octopus/picoquic"
    notes: "Vendored inside fabric-godot-core's modules/http3. The transport under WebTransportPeer."
